          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 28-year-old woman presents with a painless breast lump. As part of triple asse</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 28-year-old woman presents with a painless breast lump. As part of triple assessment, which is the most appropriate first-line imaging investigation?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Mammography</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Mammography is less effective in women under 35 because their breast tissue is dense, reducing mammographic sensitivity. It becomes the first-line imaging modality for women over 35–40 years.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Ultrasonography</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. In women under 35, breast tissue is dense, making ultrasonography the first-line imaging investigation. It reliably distinguishes solid from cystic lesions and is safe in pregnancy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Breast MRI</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Breast MRI is reserved for specific indications such as screening high-risk women with BRCA mutations or evaluating the extent of known disease. It is not the first-line investigation for a new breast lump.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>CT scan of the chest</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>CT of the chest is used for staging metastatic breast cancer, not for evaluating a primary breast lump. It forms part of the metastatic work-up, not the initial triple assessment.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "For women under 35, ultrasonography is first-line because dense breast tissue limits mammographic sensitivity. Above 35–40 years, mammography is preferred. This imaging choice is one of the three arms of triple assessment."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done! Breast density decreases with age, so ultrasonography is preferred under 35 and mammography over 35–40. Choosing the right imaging modality is a key step in triple assessment."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
