          "label": "<p>A 20-year-old woman has a 2 cm smooth, mobile breast lump. Concordant triple ass</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 20-year-old woman has a 2 cm smooth, mobile breast lump. Concordant triple assessment confirms a fibroadenoma. What is the most appropriate next step?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Immediate surgical excision</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Immediate excision is not required for a small fibroadenoma with concordant triple assessment. Surgery is considered only if the lump exceeds 3–4 cm or continues to grow.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Wide local excision with clear margins</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Wide local excision with clear margins is the treatment for a phyllodes tumour, which can recur if incompletely excised. A fibroadenoma does not require margin clearance.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Observation with follow-up</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. A small (&lt;3–4 cm) fibroadenoma confirmed by concordant triple assessment can safely be observed. Excision is indicated if it grows or exceeds 3–4 cm.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Anti-staphylococcal antibiotics and incision and drainage</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Antibiotics and drainage are the treatment for a lactational breast abscess caused by Staphylococcus aureus entering through a cracked nipple, not for a fibroadenoma.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "A small fibroadenoma confirmed by concordant triple assessment can safely be observed. Excision is indicated only if it exceeds 3–4 cm or grows. Wide excision is for phyllodes tumours; antibiotics and drainage are for lactational abscesses."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done! Concordant triple assessment provides confidence in the benign diagnosis. Small fibroadenomas are safely observed; excision is reserved for large or growing lesions."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
