          "label": "<p>A 52-year-old woman's core biopsy confirms invasive breast carcinoma. Using the </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 52-year-old woman's core biopsy confirms invasive breast carcinoma. Using the SPIKES framework, what should the surgeon do BEFORE delivering the diagnosis?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Discuss the surgical treatment plan in detail</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The treatment plan is discussed after the diagnosis has been delivered and the patient's emotional response acknowledged. Jumping to treatment before disclosure denies the patient time to process the news.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Immediately inform the patient's family members</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The diagnosis is delivered to the patient first, in a private setting with a breast-care nurse present. Family members are involved only with the patient's consent and at her pace.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Assess her perception of the situation and ask how much information she wants</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. In the SPIKES framework, Perception and Invitation come before Knowledge. The surgeon first explores what the patient already suspects, then asks how much she wants to know, before delivering the diagnosis in plain language.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Obtain written consent for mastectomy</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Consent can only be obtained after the diagnosis has been explained, treatment options discussed, and the patient has had time to ask questions. As the video emphasises, consent is a continuing dialogue, not a single signature.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Before delivering a cancer diagnosis, the SPIKES framework requires assessing the patient's Perception (what she already suspects) and Invitation (how much she wants to know). Only then is the diagnosis delivered, followed by empathy and a clear strategy."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done! SPIKES guides the difficult conversation: Setting, Perception, Invitation, Knowledge, Empathy, Strategy — each step respects the patient's readiness and autonomy."
                }
              ],
